{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791184877,"nonce":"aQvY2OhhtEm45IIbupsheewHJlw=","title":"3D: frustum culling, with meshes kept until they go or a budget is reached","message":"Skip each mesh whose bounding box is outside the camera's frustum. This saves vertex and fragment work for scenes with many models, and a mesh never in view never uploads (uploads already happen on first draw).\n\n**Prerequisite:** ScenePainter frees any mesh not drawn in a frame (endFrame). With culling, a mesh that leaves the view would be freed and uploaded again on its return. Change the rule first:\n- keep a mesh's buffers and its material group while the mesh is alive;\n- free them when it is disposed, or least-recently-drawn first past a GPU memory budget.\n\n**Plan:**\n- Bounds: MeshData already has min/max. Transform the 8 corners by the draw's transform and test them against the 6 planes taken from the view-projection (Gribb–Hartmann).\n- Cull in ScenePainter.render before writing the draws buffer, so slots stay contiguous.\n- Count culled draws, for a test and for the frame log.\n\n**Later:** occlusion culling (hi-Z), and mip streaming by on-screen size (upload only the mip levels a mesh's projected size needs), both for large scenes.\n\n**Test:** two meshes, one behind the camera. Check the draws written and the uploads made, and that turning the camera to the hidden mesh draws it without a fresh upload stall.\n\nRelated: 3D layers of canvases out of view are already freed after a second unpainted (ScenePainter.sweep).","files":null}]}